+- **A probe's verdict is computed from the observation it names, never
+  inferred from proxies** (`unprobed` — contributor incident as shape; see
+  Provenance). A harness that prints "X ran from Y" must have located Y
+  and tied X to it; deriving that line from two cheaper facts that are
+  consistent with it ("the old output appeared" and "the expected in-tree
+  file is gone") is an inference, and an inference is wrong in the
+  direction of its author's belief while printing the right answer — it
+  also hides the fixture's own bugs, because a path-resolution error
+  that makes the search find nothing looks identical to "nothing there".
+  Before trusting a verdict line, read the code that emits it and
+  confirm every noun in the line corresponds to a measured value
+  (a path the runtime reported, a digest compared, a return code
+  checked); where a noun is not measured, either measure it or weaken
+  the line to what was. And the trail that carries the verdict is
+  evidence only for what it captured: a README that asserts "`-S`
+  prints the same value" or "no env variable differs" with no
+  command-and-output pair checked in is a claim the reviewer must
+  mark unconfirmed — capture each asserted probe's invocation and
+  output at the revision the claim is about.
+  ✅ "the probe records the path the compiler returned, asserts that
+  file survived the clean, reads the imported module's own cache
+  attribute back, and prints REPRODUCED only when the two resolve to
+  the same file."
+  ❌ "OLD printed and `__pycache__` is empty → 'stale bytes ran from the
+  out-of-tree cache'" — the artifact was never located; when the search
+  was made real, a `/var` vs `/private/var` resolution bug turned out to
+  have been finding nothing all along.
